// Generalization of issue #350/#388's fix: a closure capturing a local
// declared in an OUTER try, itself inside an INNER, nested try/catch that
// throws and is caught. The trigger is the OUTER catch's own `e` binding,
// not the inner one: once the outer try's own scope closes (normally, or
// if a fault ever escaped the inner catch), `e` reuses `x`'s slot — the
// captured, still-live-via-`f` slot — even though this specific run never
// actually enters the outer catch (the inner one handles the throw).
// isFreshDeclaration (jvm_emitter.cpp) must still recognize that binding
// as a fresh declaration, because the verifier checks every catch clause
// a class contains, whether or not a given run ever reaches it. Confirmed:
// this program hits the original "VerifyError: Register N contains wrong
// type" when isFreshDeclaration is disabled.

var fns = [];
var doThrow = true;
try {
    var x = 5;
    try {
        fun f() { return x; }
        fns.append(f);
        // Gated behind a flag, not an unconditional throw: an unconditional
        // throw here would make the inner try body's own normal-completion
        // close structurally unreachable, which the capture corpus keeps
        // at zero (test_backend_capture.cpp's own CaptureAnalysisTest.
        // NoAssertionFailureOnExamples measures this across every example)
        // — see test/translation-probes/60_throw_ends_try_binding.lox's
        // own header comment for the same pattern.
        if (doThrow) { throw "inner"; }
    } catch (e) { print "inner caught " + e; }
} catch (e) { print "outer " + e; }
print fns[0]();

// CHECK: inner caught inner
// CHECK: 5
